{"type":"decision","classification":"tactical","recorded_at":"2026-07-27T00:06:36.294Z","evidence":{"seeds":"resonance-80bf","commit":"1ef8b74665a0f5f70b6634906c03977ecee999bb"},"title":"Pin design/manifest captures with a SHA-256 of design.png, and flag copy-derived provenance, while Figma REST is 403","rationale":"The manifest cites node ids from a COPY of the designer's file (vC0O5uyMmw1o5vYHmCoOXq 'Resonance (Copy)' for screens 08-12; UYlkCL7jkCVgKWiqAVlEFp for 01-07; the designer's canonical file is neither). Figma preserves node ids across a copy, so an id resolves forever while its MEANING drifts as the designer edits the original. The citation never fails — it just quietly stops describing the right frame. That is the exact self-certification failure ADR-0019 exists to prevent, reintroduced one layer down. The natural fix, recording a Figma version id per capture, is impossible because REST is 403. DECISION: record fileKey + nodeId + capturedAt + designPngSha256 + provenance:copy-derived in every design.md, and add design/manifest/PROVENANCE.md stating plainly which file the manifest trusts and what that is worth. The hash works as a drift detector because exportAsync at a fixed scale is byte-reproducible. LIMIT, stated explicitly: content hashing catches drift WITHIN the file we can see, never drift BETWEEN the copy and the canonical file — the copy is frozen, so its hash matches happily while the real design moves. Only migrating to read-only access on the canonical file fixes that, filed as resonance-6db8 with an ADR-0019 amendment. Corollary: this workflow only ever READS Figma, so a copy buys nothing and costs integrity for free — never call a Figma write tool against a design file.","id":"mx-4efe48","status":"archived","archived_at":"2026-09-11T20:48:23.522Z","archive_reason":"manual: Superseded by the ADR-0019 amendment decision (2026-09-11): the trusted source is a registered dated snapshot, not the July copies"}
